\details{
The \verb{K = } argument in most functions of the \strong{Prova} package, such as \code{Pr()}, accepts either a "K" (more precisely "prova_K") object, or a path to a directory containing a 'K.rds' file with such object, or a path to such a file itself. In a typical session or script, the "K" object was calculated beforehand, and is the same in all functions, unless the user is comparing probabilities calculated from different sampling data or different amounts of sampling data.

The function \code{useK()} allows the user to specify a "K" object to be implictily used in all \verb{K = } arguments of subsequent function calls. Then such arguments can simply be omitted, saving a lot of \code{K = ...}-typing (attention must still be paid to argument order; see details of individual functions).

This function also checks if the given \code{K} is a valid "K" object, or path to one, and throws an error otherwise. Note that any previously declared object is replaced.

(This function also saves, mostly for internal purpose in plotting functions, the name of the variable that pointed to the "K" object.)

If called with empty argument, '\code{useK()}', it returns the name of the "K" object currently set as default, or \code{NULL} if no default is set.

If called as \code{useK(NULL)} or \code{useK(NA)}, \code{useK(FALSE)}, then it declares that no default "K" objects should be used in \verb{K = } arguments any longer. Note that any subsequent calls to functions that omit \verb{K = } arguments will obviously throw an error.

The function \code{getK()} returns the actual "K" object, if its argument is a path to such an object. If the argument is the object itself it just returns it. With an empty argument, it returns the "K" object declared by \code{useK()}, throwing an error if no such object was declared. This function can be useful if you want to quickly check some information stored in the "K" object, such as the metadata or the number of Monte Carlo samples (see examples).

(This function can also return, via the \code{name} argument, the original name of the variable that pointed to the "K" object. This is mostly used for internal use of plotting functions.)
